<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">2004 Summer Olympics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Athens 2004</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2004 Summer Olympics were held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 13 to 29 August 2004, bringing the Games back to the city of the first modern Olympics of 1896. More than 10,000 athletes from 201 national Olympic committees took part. Preparations gave Athens a new airport, metro lines and a tram, but the cost of the venues added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s public debt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2004-summer-olympics.jpg" alt="2004 Summer Olympics" width="330" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FilRB · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athènes_flamme_olympique_2004small.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> had lost its bid for the 1996 Summer Olympics to Atlanta on 18 September 1990. Under the direction of Gianna Angelopoulos-Daskalaki it bid again, and the 106th IOC Session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> chose it on 5 September 1997. The bid drew on the history of the Games and was praised for its low scale, humility and focused message, unlike the 1996 bid, which had been seen as arrogant.</p><p>The Greek government spent close to 9 billion euros on the Games. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> 2004 Organizing Committee (ATHOC) finished its work as a company in 2005 with a surplus of 130.6 million euros. The Games brought a new airport, an expanded metro, a tram, a suburban railway and the Attiki Odos ring motorway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The flame was lit at Ancient Olympia on 25 March 2004 and, for the first time, travelled around the world to past and future host cities before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The opening ceremony, directed by the choreographer Dimitris Papaioannou, took place on 13 August and the Games ran until 29 August.</p><p>In all 10,625 athletes, about 600 more than expected, took part with 5,501 team officials from 201 countries, in 301 medal events in 28 sports. It was the first time since 1996 that every country with a National Olympic Committee attended. The marathon started in the town of Marathon and finished in the Panathenaic Stadium, and the shot put was held in the stadium at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> became the fourth city to host the Summer Games twice, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> led the final medal table, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> came 15th. Chile, Chinese Taipei, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and Israel won their first Olympic gold medals. The new medal obverse, designed by Elena Votsi, showed the Panathenaic Stadium, replacing a design that had depicted the Roman Colosseum.</p><p>The cost of the Games and a possible contribution to the Greek government-debt crisis of 2010–18 has been debated, mostly in popular media, though there is little or no evidence for the claim. A cost-benefit evaluation presented to the Greek Parliament in January 2013 found the net economic benefit for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> positive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “2004 Summer Olympics” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Athens 2004</b> Olympic organisers chose it as one of the most influential Greek songs of all time, and Anna Vissi sang it at the closing ceremony.</blockquote><a href="/songs/misirlou">Misirlou</a></li></ul></section>
